Date: Thu, 11 Feb 93 11:10:36 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: LAW/POLI: Tim's "Misc."

Tim Starr joins me in the busyness business.  He posts:

>the rarity of
>smokers breathing into baby's faces does little to refute that this is an
>assault: my friend Cris started smoking in precisely that manner.

Agreed, but this is oddly, perhaps deceptively, worded.  It should read
"does little to refute *the claim* that this is an assault."  And, of
course, I was not using frequency as a proxy for definition.  I was merely
pointing out that smoking is hardly ever an assault.  Also, it is unclear
how your friend could have been "started smoking" by having people breath
on him.  The tort, if any, is in deliberately threatening to expose him to
smoke.  Surely you are not claiming that others are responsible for his
ongoing habit!

>But this whole question has gotten confused on the matter of "assault,"
>a criminal charge.  

Assault is both a tort and a crime.  The same is true of battery.  My
discussion treated the matter soley as a question for tort law.  Perhaps I
should have been more explicit, but I assumed nobody was talking about
criminalization.

>The real question isn't whether the costs smokers
>externalize onto others are criminal, but whether they're torts, civil
>offenses.  

Agreed.

>Under Richard Epstein's theory of strict liability, it would
>seem that all such accidentally externalized costs would be torts, and
>those the costs are imposed upon would have just claims to legal remedy
>in the form of restitution at least (they might have claims to injunctive
>relief).

Well, it isn't quite that simple.  First of all (*gasp*), Epstein may be
wrong in claiming strict liability should rule the day.  Even some friends
of liberty prefer negligence or contributory negligence standards.  And
even under strict liability, there will be no claims in cases where people
voluntarily agree to expose themselves to smoke by entering another's
property with notice.  So there is generally no tort claim for having
sniffed cigs at a smoking-permitted restaurant, nor for breathing
second-hand smoke in another's home.  This is not contributory negligence,
but rather waiver of liability resulting from submission to others'
property rights.

>So, if smokers harm others, then those others oughta be able to sue for
>restitution and get smokers ordered by court of law to stop harming them.

Again, this is inaccurate.  Showing harm is not sufficent.  You have to
show lack of an overriding right to smoke.

>The big question here is whether any harm is inflicted, and, if so, how
>much?  

I don't think this is such a big question.  You can bring a claim for
nuisance even if another's actions are merely annoying, inconvenient, or
discomforting.  Smoke has been judged to be as much many, many times
already, albeit in other contexts.  Granted, there are some tricky factual
questions about the proper remedy, but those aren't *big* questions--they
are just tough ones.


>Now to attack Tom for his claims about collective justification.  

This is my lucky day!  Two tests in one post!  Tim knows I love a
challenge! 8-)

>Assume
>that A accepts statism, and B doesn't.  A supports coercion, and B doesn't.
>A supports coercion of B, and B doesn't support coercion of A.  Surely
>there's something wrong with saying that coercion of B is justified
>relative to A, but coercion of A isn't relative to B.  

I never said as much.  I only said that A would be justified in coercing
A', someone else who had accepted statism.  I expressly denied that A would
be justified in coercing B.   

As I pointed out, this approach has an interesting impact on statism.  But
as before, I will put off further discussion until someone addresses the
issue directly.


