Date: Thu, 11 Feb 93 10:02:15 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

Hal Finney asks: "Is the average of the betas of two "opposite"
contingent assets (with the same underlying asset) always equal to the
beta of the underlying asset?"

Let me quote from Sharpe, Investments, 3rd Ed. p.159.  "the beta of a
portfolio is simply a weighted average of the beta values of the
component securities, using the proportions invested as weights".  
The underlying asset is a portfolio of the contingent assets.  QED.  

Robin

