   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   > From: drw@bourbaki.mit.edu
   > 
   > OK, I conceed.  It's not like it matters to the point.  The point is
   > that people who are *trying* to make a profit will (toward the end of
   > a development boom) build a significant number of projects that are
   > "unneeded" in that the space they contain is unsalable.

   I'm not going to let you get away with this one, either. I want to hear
   references that say that a significant percentage of projects are unprofitable.
   This has to be a time-based study, by the way, as anomalous periods like
   recessions tend to make lots of normally profitable activities temporarily
   unprofitable. If you can find me real evidence that market mechanisms
   don't work and that developers will continue to build malls long past
   the point at which they start losing money on them, I'd be rather suprised,
   sufficiently that I want to see hard evidence.

The short answer is "the last three years of the Boston Globe".

Development projects have been going broke here ever since the Great
Real-Estate Crash, which started around 1989.  Since a sizable
fraction of the projects had large mortgages from various banks (i.e.,
they laid off a significant fraction of the risk onto the banks),
banks have been crashing down with disturbing regularity.  And a
considerable number of previously-rich developers are now broke.

Of course, you can blame it on the recession, but that's a bit
backwards, here:  it's the real-estate crash that *caused* (or
perhaps, detonated) the recession here in the Northeast.

The underlying problem is rather simple, really; it takes at least a
couple of years to bring a building on the market after you get the
financing arranged and the construction started.  That is, there's a
long time delay between the no-turning-back point and when the
building is available for leasing.  The market can turn from tight to
loose in that length of time.  If the developers were perfectly
rational, they would carefully project out the demand and supply, and
build only if there was a predictable shortfall.  But toward the end
of a long boom, a lot of people with more money than brains get into
the game, as developers and as financers.

A lot of buyers of residential real estate got caught in the same
bind.  As I was told in 1987, "Invest in real estate!  It can't
possibly go down!"  Yeah, right.

Dale

