   In the case of slavery, this is different.  If Jones allegedly
   contracts with Smith to become his slave, then, after the surrender
   of all moral and legal standing, who gets to "stand on" the terms
   of the contract?

Though I don't think it's necessary that the slave relinquish all
legal standing.  That may have been the theory in U.S. slavery, but my
impression is that slavery in most of "classical" times was more like
a permanent employment contract or indentured servitude (compare
apprenticeship!).  I've seen reports that slaves sometimes owned
slaves in their own right, and possessed personal property.  And the
relationship between master and slave was rather tightly regulated by
a body of customary law, and seems to have been enforceable against
masters by the current legal systems.

(In Massachusetts, a law was passed sometime in the colonial period
forbidding masters from feeding lobster to their indentured servants
more than twice a week.  Apparently a law was considered necessary
because an indentured servant didn't have the recourse of quitting.)

